.TH GLIMT 1 "2026-11-10" "Glimt 0.79.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glimt \- fast, lightweight image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glimt
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE_OR_FOLDER " ...]"
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-remote
.I COMMAND
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-remote\-all
.I COMMAND
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-help
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-version
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-action\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Glimt
shows the images of a folder, one at a time, with a file list beside them. It is inspired by
.BR geeqie (1)
and uses its keyboard shortcuts, but focuses on speed and little memory: the images on each
side of the shown one are decoded in advance at window size, and folders with hundreds of
thousands of files list as quickly as small ones.
.PP
Given a file, Glimt shows it with the rest of its folder; given a folder, its first image;
given a collection
.RI ( .gqv )
or a plain list of images
.RI ( .txt ", " .m3u :
one path on each line, relative to the list or absolute,
.B #
for comments), those images in their order, the list never written;
given nothing, the folder used last (if that is set in Preferences) or the current folder.
Given several folders, Glimt shows the images of all of them as one list, for example for a
slideshow across them; random order mixes them all.
A URI such as
.I smb://server/share
is mounted with
.BR gio (1)
first. Leave a password out of it: written here, it is in Glimt's own command line, which
other users of the computer can read while Glimt runs. Left out, Glimt asks for it in its
login dialog, as when the address is typed in the folder field.
.PP
It reads JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF (also animated), TIFF (also with several pages), BMP, ICO
(each size as a page), QOI, PNM (PBM, PGM, PPM, PAM), Radiance HDR (tone-mapped) and TIFF
under other names (Zeiss LSM, gel scans; also grey TIFFs of floats, stretched), and camera RAW
files by their embedded preview. Builds with optional features also read videos, HEIC, AVIF, JPEG XL and SVG. Other
formats (DICOM, FITS, microscope files …) are read through formats you add (see FILES).
.SH OPTIONS
The options that start Glimt a certain way
.RB ( \-\-slideshow ", " \-\-random ", " \-\-fullscreen ", " \-\-kiosk ,
.BR \-\-lock\-settings ", " \-\-layout ", " \-\-fade " and " \-\-name )
may also come after the files and folders, as in
.BR "glimt a.jpg \-\-fullscreen" ,
as Geeqie reads its options.
.TP
.B \-\-slideshow
Start the slideshow once the folder is read. Given a collection
.RI ( .gqv )
or a plain list of images
.RI ( .txt ", " .m3u :
one path on each line, relative to the list or absolute,
.B #
for comments), it plays them in the list's order, for example
.BR "glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-fullscreen show.txt" .
.TP
.B \-\-random
Show the slideshow in random order. Kept for later starts, as in the View menu.
.TP
.B \-\-fullscreen
Start in full screen.
.TP
.B \-\-kiosk
Full screen with only the image, for a picture screen: the keys and the mouse browse, zoom,
change the layout and run the slideshow, but there are no menus or panels, nothing changes
files or settings beyond these, and Escape, Q, Ctrl+Q and closing the window are refused.
Only
.B glimt \-\-remote quit
(or
.BR \-\-remote\-all )
ends it; where remote control cannot work, without
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
Ctrl+Q does. The desktop's own keys (switching windows, logging out) still work; to keep them
away, run Glimt as the only program of a kiosk session, for example
.B cage \-\- glimt \-\-kiosk \-\-slideshow
.I ~/Pictures
or as the program of a GNOME Kiosk session.
.TP
.B \-\-lock\-settings
For a shared or public computer: Preferences do not open, the sort manager's folders stay as
they are, View \(-> Theme neither saves a theme nor opens a folder or the browser, and nothing
is written to the settings file, so what is changed in the menus (the
theme, the sort order, the zoom mode) lasts only until Glimt closes. Marks, keywords and
collections are kept as always. An administrator can lock every Glimt on the computer by
creating the file
.IR /etc/glimt/lock\-settings ;
in a Flatpak, which does not see the computer's
.IR /etc ,
use the option.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout " LAYOUT"
Show several images at once, as View \(-> Layout:
.B 1
(one image),
.B 2x1
(side by side),
.B 1x2
(above each other) or
.B 2x2
(four). Kept for later starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-fade " off|short|long"
How the slideshow goes from one image to the next: at once, or fading over 0.4 or 1 second.
Kept for later starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Name this Glimt: letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ ,
at most 64 bytes (fewer in a Flatpak, whose socket path is longer). The
window title shows it, and
.B glimt \-\-name
.I NAME
.B \-\-remote
.I COMMAND
sends
.I COMMAND
to the Glimt windows of that name only (with
.BR open ,
it starts one with that name if none runs). Several windows may share a name, and each of
them does what is sent to it; a query has an answer from each. A named Glimt listens even
when remote control is off in Preferences. With
.BR \-\-remote ,
.I NAME
may also be the process ID of a Glimt started without a name, or of one that shares its
name with a Glimt started before it, as
.B identify
and
.B %id%
show it; a name of only digits is taken for nothing else.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " COMMAND"
Send
.I COMMAND
to the Glimt window used last, on this computer, and wait for its answer. Exits with 0 when
the command was done; with 2 when it cannot be sent: a command Glimt does not know or with the
wrong arguments, a
.B run
of an action that is not for scripts, more than 100,000 files, or a path that is not UTF-8 or
has a line break in it; with 1 when no Glimt runs or
none answers within 10 seconds (for each file of
.BR info ),
and with 3 when the window did not do it: a
.B run
refused while a dialog asks the user or a text field has the focus, by a kiosk, or with no
image to act on, or a command that window does not know, as an older Glimt; the reason is printed on standard error. A Glimt older than
0.76.0 answers nothing to a command, which counts as done.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-all " COMMAND"
Send
.I COMMAND
to every running Glimt of this user on this computer. Exits as
.B \-\-remote
does, with 3 when any of them did not do it, also when one did not answer within 10 seconds
while another answered, and with 1 only when none answered.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
List the options and remote commands.
.TP
.BI "\-\-check\-config [" FILE ]
Tell what is wrong in the settings file, or in
.IR FILE ,
line by line: a line that is not
.IR setting = value ,
a setting that does not exist or is set twice, and a value that cannot be read, is out of
range or is not one of the setting's choices (such as
.B print_paper=letter
for
.BR Letter ),
with the value used instead, and an own key in
.B keys=
that names no action or a key that cannot be read (such as
.BR Next=ctrl+N ). Prints OK and exits with 0 when nothing is wrong, and also
when there is no settings file yet, as Glimt then uses the defaults; else exits with 1, also
when the file cannot be read or a
.I FILE
given is not there. When it starts, Glimt tells the first of these
in its window, but not a setting it does not know, which a newer Glimt beside it may have
written. Under a heading of its own, it also tells each added format or tool whose
.B Exec
has
.B %f
only inside a quoted word, such as
.BR "sh -c \(dqconvert %f ppm:-\(dq" :
such a format is not loaded and such a tool gets no files. Give
.B %f
as a word of its own, and the script reads it as
.BR \(dq$1\(dq :
.BR "sh -c \(dqconvert \e\(dq$1\e\(dq ppm:-\(dq sh %f" .
These do not change the exit code, which tells only about the settings file.
.TP
.BI "\-\-back\-up " FOLDER
Copy Glimt's own folders (settings, marks and stars, collections, keyword tree, tools and
added formats; not the thumbnails) to a new folder
.I glimt\-backup\-DATE
in
.IR FOLDER ,
as File \(-> Back Up Glimt's Data does, without opening a window: for cron or a systemd
timer. A second backup the same day goes beside the first. Prints where it went and exits with
0; exits with 2 without
.IR FOLDER ,
and with 1
when
.I FOLDER
is no folder or lies inside Glimt's own folders, when there is nothing to back up, or when a
file could not be copied.
.TP
.B \-\-action\-list
Print the actions that
.B \-\-remote run
takes, one to a line: the action's name, a tab and its menu label in English, such as
.B ZoomIn
and
.BR "Zoom In" .
Scripts may read this list, so its form stays the same. Exits with 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options: every argument after it is a file or folder, also one whose name starts with
.BR \- ,
such as
.BR "glimt \-\- \-h.jpg" .
.SH REMOTE COMMANDS
Remote commands reach only the Glimt windows of the same user on the same computer: they go
through a Unix socket in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/glimt ,
which only that user can open. Glimt opens no network port. To control Glimt on another
computer, run the command there, for example
.BR "ssh you@laptop glimt \-\-remote next" .
.TP
.BR next ", " previous ", " first ", " last
Show the next, previous, first or last image.
The old word
.BR prev ,
from before 0.74.0, is taken too.
.TP
.BR slideshow " [on|off]"
Start or stop the slideshow; without the word, the other of the two.
.TP
.BR random " [on|off]"
Random order on or off; without the word, the other.
.TP
.BR fullscreen " [on|off]"
Enter or leave full screen; without the word, the other.
.TP
.BR subfolders " on|off"
Include the images of the subfolders in the list, or not, as View \(-> Include Subfolders;
kept, as the menu's.
.TP
.BI open " PATH"
Show a file, folder or collection, or mount a network location such as
.IR smb://nas/photos ,
as Glimt does with one on its command line. If no Glimt is running, one is started.
The commands sent after it wait until the place has been looked up, except
.BR quit .
A further
.B open
replaces the look-up, and the commands that waited for it are not done.
.TP
.BI title " [TEXT ...]"
Give this window a title of its own, a template as in Preferences of
.BR %name% ", " %path% ", " %folder% ", " %app% " and " %id% ,
until it closes; without the text, the one in Preferences again.
.TP
.BI name " [NAME]"
Give this window a new name, as
.B \-\-name
does at start, so that
.B \-\-remote
reaches it by that name from now on and no longer by the old one; it is shared when another
window has it. Without
.IR NAME ,
the window has no name, and its process ID reaches it. A window that did not listen, with
remote control off, starts to when it is given a name, as with
.B \-\-name
at start, and stops again when its name is taken away. Window \(-> Set Window Name… does
the same.
.TP
.B identify
Show this window's ID large over the image for a few seconds: its
.B \-\-name
or else its process ID, as
.B %id%
in the title. A window that shares its name with one started before it shows its own ID
beside the name, as
.BR "left (4242)" ,
and
.B \-\-name 4242
reaches it alone. With
.BR \-\-remote\-all ,
each window shows its own.
.TP
.B quit
Close Glimt.
.TP
.BI run " ACTION"
Do an action by its name, as in the
.B keys=
setting and Preferences \(-> Keyboard, for example
.B run ZoomIn
or
.BR "run ThumbnailGrid" .
Only the actions that change no file, sidecar, mark or collection, not even through a window
the user then clicks in, are taken (showing, browsing, selecting, the panels and windows, the
settings and the clipboard), and none while a dialog asks the user or a text field has the focus;
.B glimt \-\-action\-list
lists them. An action that was renamed is still taken by its old name, here and in
.BR keys= ,
such as
.B Up
for
.B ParentFolder
or
.B ExifRotate
for
.BR AutoRotate ;
the list shows only the new names. Any other name exits with 2, and a
.B run
refused exits with 3.
.TP
.B current
Print the path of the image shown.
.TP
.B selection
Print the paths of the selected files, or of the image shown when none is selected. When the
search hides every selected file, nothing is printed.
.TP
.BI select " PATH ..."
Select these files of the list (at most 100,000) and show the first; print those that are not
in it. Sent right after
.BR open ,
it waits until the folder is read, up to ten seconds; after that nothing is printed and the exit
status is 1, so a script can try again. When the search hides one of the files, nothing is
selected and every path is printed. It only selects: deleting or moving is left to the user.
.TP
.BI info " [FILE ...]"
Print what Glimt knows about the image shown, or about the files named, listed or not (at most
100,000). Each file is a record of lines of a key, a tab and a value, and a blank line ends it.
The keys are always all there, in this order, with an empty value where the file has none:
.BR path ,
.B size
(in bytes),
.B modified
(RFC 3339, in UTC),
.B width
and
.B height
(as stored, before the orientation),
.B format
(such as
.BR png ,
from the file's first bytes, or else its extension),
.B orientation
(the EXIF value, 1 to 8),
.B stars
(0 to 5) and
.B marks
(such as
.BR 1,4 ),
which are empty without marks built in,
.B keywords
(joined by
.BR "; " ,
with a
.B ;
inside a keyword written as
.BR \e; ),
.BR comment ,
.BR alt_text ,
.BR extended_description ,
and the info panel's EXIF fields, in English:
.BR exif.camera ,
.BR exif.lens ,
.BR exif.taken ,
.BR exif.exposure ,
.BR exif.aperture ,
.BR exif.iso ,
.BR exif.focal_length ,
.BR exif.exposure_bias ,
.B exif.flash
and
.BR exif.gps .
In a value, a backslash is printed as
.BR \e\e ,
a tab as
.BR \et ,
a line feed as
.B \en
and a carriage return as
.BR \er ,
so each value stays on its line and a script can undo it. The path is printed with its own
bytes, as
.B current
prints it, also when they are not UTF-8. A path that is no file has every value but its path
empty. Each file is printed as soon as it is read, and the 10 seconds that the client waits
hold for each file, not for all of them. When the window closes before every file is printed,
it exits with 3. The form stays the same in 1.x, but a later Glimt may add keys, so a script
reads a value by its key. Without a file named and no image shown, it exits with 3.
.PP
.BR current ,
.B selection
and
.B select
print each path followed by a NUL byte, as
.B find \-print0
does, since a file name may hold a line break: read them with
.B xargs \-0
.RB "or " "read \-d ''" ,
not line by line. No remote command deletes, moves or renames a file.
.SH KEYS
The keys are Geeqie's; Help \(-> Keyboard Shortcuts lists them all, and Preferences can change
them. The most used:
.TP
.BR Space ", " "Page Down" ", " \(->
Next image.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " "Page Up" ", " \(<-
Previous image.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
First and last image.
.TP
.BR Z ", " X ", " + ", " \-
Zoom 1:1, zoom to fit, zoom in and out.
.TP
.BR F ", " F11 ", " Esc
Enter and leave full screen.
.TP
.BR S ", " P ", " R
Start or stop the slideshow, pause it, random order.
.TP
.BR ] ", " [
Rotate the view clockwise and counterclockwise.
.TP
.B Delete
Move the selected files, or the shown one, to the trash; in a collection, take them out of it.
.TP
.BR C ", " O
New collection of the selected files; open a collection.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+K ", " Alt+K
The info panel, with the keywords and comment; Alt+K to edit the keywords.
.TP
.B Ctrl+P
Print the selected images, or the shown one, to a printer or a PDF file.
.TP
.B Shift+S
The sort manager: folders that the selected files are copied or moved to with a click.
.TP
.B Ctrl+J
Pan view: the images by day, as a timeline.
.TP
.B D
Find duplicates among the images in the list.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Preferences.
.TP
.B F1
Help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/config
Preferences, as key=value lines (default
.IR ~/.config/glimt/config ).
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/sort-folders
The sort manager's folders, one on each line.
.TP
.I /etc/glimt/lock\-settings
While this file is there (empty will do), every Glimt starts as with
.BR \-\-lock\-settings .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/themes
Own themes, one
.I name.theme
file each, of
.I key=value
lines.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/keywords
The keyword tree: a path such as
.I Places|Norway|Oslo
on each line.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/state
The last folder and the window size and position (default
.IR ~/.local/state/glimt/state ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/recent
File \(-> Open Recent's folders and collections, newest first, one on each line.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/sharing
What File \(-> Copy for Sharing left out the last time, one word on each line.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/marks
Marks and stars, in builds with the marks feature.
.TP
.IB file .xmp
Keywords, comment, alt text and extended description, and (if chosen) stars of
.IR file ,
next to it, read by darktable and digiKam too.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/tools
Tools (Edit \(-> Tools): desktop files whose command runs on the selected files (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/tools ).
The folder's old name,
.IR plugins ,
is still read.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/renames
A Rename Many while it runs, so that one cut short by a crash is put right at the next start.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/formats
Added formats: desktop files whose command turns a file of a format Glimt does not read itself
into an image on its standard output (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/formats ).
The folder's old name,
.IR decoders ,
is still read.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/collections
Collections, as Geeqie .gqv files (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/collections ).
.TP
.IR $XDG_DATA_DIRS/glimt/tools " and " $XDG_DATA_DIRS/glimt/formats
Tools and added formats for every user, read after the user's own (default
.I /usr/local/share
and
.IR /usr/share ).
A file of the same name in the user's folder hides the system's.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/thumbnails
Thumbnails, shared with other programs as the freedesktop specification says.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/glimt
The sockets for remote control,
.IR glimt\-NAME.sock ,
and
.IR focused ,
the name of the window that had the focus last.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the files above are kept.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_DIRS
The system's folders that tools and added formats are read from after the user's own.
.TP
.BR LANGUAGE ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
The language of the interface while the language setting is empty, as gettext reads them:
the first of LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES and LANG that is set, or the list in LANGUAGE when that
locale is not C.
.TP
.B GLIMT_FORMAT_MEMORY_MB
Set by Glimt for an added format's program: the most memory, in MB, that it and what it starts
may take (Preferences \(-> Memory and CPU, the setting
.BR added_format_memory_mb ,
4096 by default). A program such as Java keeps its heap under it.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGTERM and SIGHUP close the window as Ctrl+Q does: Glimt waits while files are copied, moved
or saved, then writes its state and ends. A second signal stops the files after the one at
hand, and a third ends Glimt at once, whatever it waits for, with exit status 143; its state
is not written then. SIGKILL always ends it at once. A signal ignored when
Glimt starts stays ignored, so
.B nohup glimt
outlives its terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures
Show the images in ~/Pictures.
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures/holiday/beach.jpg
Show that image, with the rest of its folder to browse.
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures/2024 ~/Pictures/2025
Both folders as one list, for one slideshow across them.
.TP
.B glimt smb://nas/photos
A folder on a network share, mounted with gio first.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-layout 2x2 ~/Pictures
Four images at once.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-random \-\-fullscreen ~/Pictures
A slideshow in random order on the whole screen.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-fullscreen \-\-fade long ~/show.txt
The images listed in show.txt, one path on each line, in that order, fading from one to the next.
.TP
.B printf '%s\en' ~/Pictures/*.jpg > ~/show.txt
Make such a list of a folder, to put in the order you want in an editor.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-kiosk \-\-slideshow ~/Pictures
A picture frame: only the image, nothing that changes files; ends with glimt \-\-remote quit.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-name left ~/a & glimt \-\-name right ~/b
Two windows that scripts can tell apart: glimt \-\-name left \-\-remote next reaches the first only.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote next
Go to the next image in the Glimt used last, for example from a desktop shortcut.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote open ~/Pictures/holiday
Show another folder in the running Glimt, or start one.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote select ~/Pictures/*sunset*
Select those images in the list; the ones not in it are printed.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote selection | xargs \-0 cp \-t ~/best
Copy the selected images to ~/best, also when a name holds a space or a line break.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote current | xargs \-0 exiftool \-Model
The camera of the image shown.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote info | awk \-F '\et' '$1 == \(dqkeywords\(dq { print $2 }'
The keywords of the image shown.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote\-all quit
Close every Glimt.
